<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>So I appreciate all the work going into revising this, and for the most part you had me&#8230; and then I got to the end:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Sir Reginald's mental state improves, but the various other characters manifested from the SCP-A-2 event can't cope with the nature of their reality either, killing themselves. Sir Reginald enters a delusional state after his last friend dies, interacting with thin air as if his friends still existed. The article ends with a note from Sir Reginald, explaining that he tried everything, but he couldn't take it anymore, with Sir Reginald killing himself too in one moment of lucidity.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Can I suggest that him merely “losing his mind” and then wanting to kill himself is a weak way to explore someone’s difficulty in coming to terms with the fact that he used to he a fictional character? It’s just a bit tropey, and could be much more haunting if he’s just having to accept his lot over a slow process of accepting what’s happened&#8230;</p>
<p>Additionally, I think the introduction of the other fictional characters make a distracting element. Focus on on Reginald, and his grappling with this situation.</p>
<p>No one says this has to end in tragedy of such a dramatic nature&#8230; consider how sad it is for someone in his position to come to grips with his unreality, and now being fully aware, would be contained for the foreseeable future&#8230;</p>
<p>You could utilize psychoanalytic therapy sessions to explore his feelings on the matter and that would be a killer article. Therefore shifting away from the importance of the crazy circumstances and instead telling a grounded story of someone trying to understand who they are.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

