			<div class="content" id="post-content-4863975">
									

<p>Okay so I see you've revised the ending based on Grigori's feedback, which I agree with wholeheartedly. But right now, the revised ending is left a little open and unsatisfying.</p>
<p>I know your intention was to leave the answers to those questions unanswered (particularly because they can't be answered easily in the first place), but I think you should still consider <em>something</em> that happens to Sir Reginald that brings his story to a close.</p>
<p>Consider two possible options to end the article:<br />
(1) Sir Reginald takes his own life. Yes, we'd usually discourage this, but if you can justify his actions / sadness / reasons for doing so, it's an entirely plausible ending. I like that interaction between 1 and 2 - your last &quot;shot&quot; or entry, so to speak, could be to show the TV-Reginald smiling, back in his own reality. Even though he killed himself in the real world, he's finally found his own happiness back in the television show.</p>
<p>(2) Sir Reginald finds a NEW purpose in the real world. Maybe he helps the Foundation contain other fictional characters that come to life? Maybe he becomes a consultation on his own television show. Readers do want some element of closure, but which ending you choose will depend on the message you wish to convey to the audience.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Does he have a soul? Is he human? Does he have a purpose? If he can exists in two worlds, how does he know that his current level of existence is real? Is he real?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I've tried to align the possible endings above to your guiding central questions. But it's really your choice at the end of the day. What you have right now don't seem to tackle those themes as much as I'd like.</p>

