			<div class="content" id="post-content-4860721">
									

<p><strong>Summoned via PM:</strong></p>
<p>Hi there EagleKnight9. Let’s take a look at your concept.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>So I do like the original premise of fictional characters coming to life. There are, however, details in the narrative that are either confusing or unplausible. For example:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The Foundation doesn't realize that he is a fictional character</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is highly unlikely, as it would simply take a Google search to identify him. You also mentioned that the character is from a “popular kid’s show”, and I’d assume his mannerisms and dress-sense would mirror that of his character, making him readily identifiable.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>He slowly regains a sense of self awareness throughout the article due to this, slowly regaining memories and gradually realizing what he is, going insane.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The process by which this occurs isn’t fleshed out. So what are you trying to say here: that upon first manifestation, he only has the memories of… what? His life in the TV show? Then, the longer he stays alive in our reality, he … what? Becomes a real person? All this isn’t clear.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Sir Reginald starts doubting his own existence, causing him to flicker in an out of reality occasionally, sometimes gaining the characteristics of someone in a 2D world.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Why would this happen? If he’s gaining self-awareness and becoming more “human”, why is he fading out of reality? Similarly, what does “gaining the characteristics of someone in a 2D world” mean exactly?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The article ends with Sir Reginald killing himself inside his cell, due to being unable to cope with his state of &quot;unreality&quot;.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Again, you need to have a firmer grip on the details here. If he’s already “flickering” out of reality, why does he need to kill himself?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Hook: The premise of a SCP slowly losing its mind due to it being foreign to the universe and its rules.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The hook here is good. You’re trying to portray a fictional character as a real-life human being. You just need to clarify the rules of this transition so that it’s more believable.</p>
<p>As a final note, I don’t really like the idea of Sir Reginald being an additional reality-bender. That’s a bit too much power, right? If you do go down this route, you’re essentially saying <em>every</em> fictional character that manifests into our reality is <em>also</em> a reality-bender. If that’s the story you want to tell, sure. But the rules, my friend, are key to a consistent narrative. Once you decide on what they are, the rest of the story will fall into place.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>If you decide to revise the concept / respond below, do drop me another PM and I’ll do my best to take another look. All the best!</p>

